  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1028 PM CDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\nPost-frontal, drier air continues to advect into the region,\nsupporting a cooler, more comfortable night across the Mid-South.\nTemperatures range from the low 70s to 80 degrees, with dewpoints\ndropping into the mid-60s north of I-40. Near the stalled\nboundary over northeast Mississippi, elevated moisture persists;\nweak upper-level easterly flow overriding the boundary will\nsupport continued isolated shower activity overnight. Tomorrow\nmorning will feature comfortable conditions, with lows in the 60s\nalong and north of I-40 and lower 70s to the south. TS Bertha\nremains near the Gulf Coast, maintaining a slow westward\ntrajectory toward Texas.\n\nBroad northwest flow aloft will persist across the Mid-South from\nThursday through Saturday, keeping temperatures slightly below\nclimatological normals through Friday. Dry conditions are\nexpected for the majority of the area. However, northeast\nMississippi remains the exception, with diurnally driven\nconvection likely along the stalled frontal boundary.\n\nThe boundary begins to retreat northward late Friday, leading to\na rise in moisture and instability across the region. A shortwave\ntraversing the Lower Mississippi Valley on Friday will catalyze\nrenewed shower and thunderstorm activity. The REFS highlights a\ndecaying MCS approaching the Mid-South late Friday night, with\nthe potential for evolution into an MCV on Saturday. LREF\nguidance indicates approximately 1500 J/kg of SBCAPE by Saturday\nafternoon with weak bulk shear around 15 knots. While model\nconfidence in specific timing remains low, convective coverage\nand probability continue to trend upward with subsequent model\nruns.\n\nThe primary forecast concern for Sunday transitions to an\namplifying subtropical ridge building east into the Lower\nMississippi Valley, promoting a significant increase in heat and\nhumidity. NBM 50th percentile apparent temperatures suggest heat\nindices reaching 105 degrees, beginning Sunday and persisting \ninto early next week. An Omega blocking pattern is forecast to \ndevelop across the CONUS by mid-week. The potential for some \nrelief from oppressive heat arrives late next week as the ridge \namplifies westward, allowing for a transition to northwest flow \nover the Lower Mississippi Valley.\n\nAC3",
